---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('klaviyo')!;
const b = appBySlug('omnisend')!;
const path = '/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Which platform wins?",
    "answer": "There is no demonstrated winner here. Compare event coverage, exclusions and maintenance using the same shopper paths in your store."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should cart and checkout run together?",
    "answer": "They may cover different stages, but later-stage activity and purchases must stop conflicting earlier reminders. Verify this in each workflow."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are the calculations observed recovery results?",
    "answer": "No. The $588 and $121 examples assume fixed product costs and illustrate how discounts reduce gross profit."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should costs be compared?",
    "answer": "Request current prices for the same audience, volume, channels and required features, then include maintenance work and messaging costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Klaviyo vs Omnisend: who owns each abandonment stage?</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Compare event coverage and the work your team must maintain. The proposed checks below are an evaluation plan, not performed supplier tests.</p>
    <TldrBox picks={[{ label: "Evaluate", tool: a.name, reason: "Inspect Shopify metrics and flow filters" },{ label: "Context", tool: b.name, reason: "Inspect cart and checkout workflow boundaries" }]} />
    <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>{a.name}</th><th>{b.name}</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Flow model</td><td>Inspect Shopify metrics and flow filters</td><td>Inspect cart and checkout workflow boundaries</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stage transition</td><td>Verify later checkout suppresses cart reminders</td><td>Verify checkout exits the earlier cart workflow</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Operating decision</td><td>Can your team maintain metric-specific branches?</td><td>Can your team maintain stage exits across workflows?</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Compare event semantics, not template names</h2>
      <p>Klaviyo distinguishes Shopify Added to Cart from Checkout Started. Omnisend documents separate abandoned cart and abandoned checkout automations. A template labelled cart recovery is not proof that both stages are covered. Write down each entry event, identification requirement, delay, exit condition and message owner before comparing designs.</p>
      <h2>Klaviyo: inspect the metric and content together</h2>
      <p>Use the Shopify metric actually arriving in the profile. An Added to Cart message needs a product block that matches that event; do not assume checkout content will work unchanged. Verify the Placed Order filter and exclusion when a shopper progresses to checkout. If old custom and current Shopify metrics coexist, trace which one triggers each branch and prevent duplicate enrollment.</p>
      <h2>Omnisend: make stage ownership explicit</h2>
      <p>Review Omnisend’s separate cart and checkout presets and their exit conditions. Assign checkout messaging to the later stage and verify that the earlier workflow stops when checkout starts; verify purchase stops recovery. Identification and tracking affect who can enter. Test an anonymous visit, an identified cart and a checkout separately rather than inferring coverage from the total audience count.</p>
      <h2>Account for documented Omnisend limits</h2>
      <p>Omnisend documents that removed cart items can remain in reminders, and an email-address change or multiple sessions can prevent the original workflow from exiting after purchase. Its documentation also says first-time abandoned carts cannot receive SMS before the phone number syncs after a first order. Include these cases in acceptance checks and disable competing Shopify reminders explicitly; enabling a new workflow does not disable them for you.</p>
      <h2>Run the same acceptance paths in both candidates</h2>
      <p>For a mobile shopper, add two variants then change quantity before checkout: check product, quantity and destination link. Start checkout while the first reminder waits: record which queue owns the next message. Purchase before the delay ends: inspect the exclusion. Repeat with an out-of-stock variant and an email-only profile. These proposed checks produce evidence for your store; no pass rate is claimed here.</p>
      <h2>Compare incentives and channel collisions</h2>
      <p>For an illustrative $588 cart at 72% gross margin, fixed product cost is $164.64. A 14% code reduces revenue from $588 to $505.68 and gross profit from $423.36 to $341.04, losing $82.32 before other costs. For a $121 cart at 41%, a 14% code leaves $32.67 instead of $49.61. Choose incentive eligibility from the actual order economics, not a universal AOV threshold. Check welcome-code stacking and overlap with campaigns before adding SMS.</p>
      <h2>Choose the maintainable design</h2>
      <p>Prefer the candidate whose event traces, exclusions and editing workflow your team can explain and maintain. Price the same billable audience, sending volume and required channels using current quotes; no rating or entry price decides this comparison. Set delays and frequency rules from the store’s purchase cycle and measured delivery behavior. An eligible SMS branch still requires its own permission, quiet-hour and queue checks.</p>
      <h2>Documentation checked November 25, 2026</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/35510464880027">Klaviyo Added to Cart flow</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005080447">Klaviyo Shopify data reference</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/6659889-abandoned-cart-and-abandoned-checkout-automations">Omnisend cart and checkout automations</a></li>
      </ul>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
